 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training is slower than supervised methods due to the alternating optimization of two networks (generator and discriminator) [S1, S2]. The multi-scale discriminator adds computational overhead proportional to $N_s$ [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires memory for both generator and discriminator networks. The discriminator is removed at test time, so inference memory matches the generator alone [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires memory for both generator and discriminator networks during training; the discriminator is removed at test time, so inference memory matches the generator alone [S2].
 *   **Performance:** Produces more realistic textures and fewer artifacts than pure L1/L2 loss methods, particularly in high-frequency details [S2]. FID scores are commonly used to evaluate quality [S3].
 *   **Strengths:** Improves generalization on unseen inputs and handles insufficient point cloud information better than non-adversarial methods [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Training instability is a risk; requires careful tuning of hyperparameters like $\lambda$ and learning rates [S3]. Not given in the sources: specific hardware scaling factors.
